# Studio audio — no self-playback

Hearing yourself (or your keyboard) in the studio is a **feedback loop**. This has been broken more than once. Do not "fix playback" by mixing local audio into speakers.

## Invariant

Studio must **hear other people, never you**.

| Path | Audio |
|---|---|
| `VideoTile` / program preview compositor | **Video tracks only.** `mixAudio: false`. Never attach mic tracks to `<video>`. `muted` on a full camera+mic stream is **not** enough — off-DOM elements still leak to speakers. |
| `useRemoteAudio` / `createRemoteAudioPlayer` | Remote peer mics only. **Never** pass `localStream` or the local peer id. |
| Headless recorder (`CompositorPage`) | `mixAudio: true` into a `MediaStreamDestination` for MediaRecorder/RTMP. That mix is a **recording sink**, not speakers. |

## Also required

**Everyone hears everyone else**, including whoever joined the room first. The first
speaker must still receive `newProducer` and consume later peers. A fix that mutes or
drops a session to kill feedback is wrong — it breaks inbound audio for that peer.

## Banned

- `createCompositor({ mixAudio: true })` in studio preview (`useProgramPreview`)
- `video.srcObject = stream` when `stream` still has audio tracks
- Feeding `localStream` into `useRemoteAudio` / `createRemoteAudioPlayer`
- Connecting the compositor mix bus to `audioContext.destination` in the studio tab
- **Closing/replacing an SFU peer session because another peer has the same `userId`
  ("one speaker per user per room" in `signaling.gateway.ts`).** Reverted in
  `git revert` of the `Fixed the feedback` attempt: it silenced the room owner's
  inbound audio while participants and the YouTube mix kept working.

## If someone reports hearing themselves / typing

The local mic is reaching speakers. Usual causes: full `MediaStream` bound to a media
element, studio `mixAudio: true`, or a duplicate producer of the same track. Do not add
a "monitor" of the local mic to "fix" it, and do not kick peer sessions to fix it.

Still open: feedback was **not** fixed by the reverted change, so the source is not a
duplicate SFU session. Before changing anything, reproduce and capture:

- SFU log lines around `joined room` / `left room` for the reporting user (how many
  peers/producers share a name, and whether any peer leaves unexpectedly)
- browser console `[sfu] consume failed` / `[remote-audio] play failed`
- which peer ids appear in `remotePeers` vs `localPeerId` on the affected client
